# FPMS-OS numpy 2.x ceiling.
#
# THIS FILE DOES NOT REPLACE /etc/apt/preferences.d/fpms-numpy. It closes the
# one hole that one has.
#
# scripts/20-python-deps.sh writes fpms-numpy, which pins the versions
# matching `1:1.*` to priority 1001. That is the right pin and its 1001 (above
# 1000, so a DOWNGRADE is permitted) is deliberate - read the comment there
# before touching either file. What it cannot do is protect against the
# absence of a 1.x: a pin only assigns a priority to versions that EXIST. If
# an archive ever offers python3-numpy 2.x and no 1.x at all - a jammy-updates
# rebuild, a PPA an operator adds, a base image that moves on - then the 1001
# pin matches nothing, the 2.x sits at the default 500, the installed 1.21.5
# sits at 100, and `apt upgrade` walks it straight over the top. Silently.
#
# WHY THAT MATTERS MORE THAN IT SOUNDS
# ROS Humble's C extensions are built against the NumPy 1.x ABI. A 2.x breaks
# tf_transformations with "np.maximum_sctype was removed in the NumPy 2.0
# release" - reported from a module nowhere near the cause - and
# selftest/verify_image.sh already fails an image whose numpy is 2.x because
# it "would break every ROS C extension in the image".
#
# A CEILING IS THE RIGHT SHAPE. Naming versions that must never be installed
# needs no knowledge of what the archive currently offers, so it keeps working
# on the day the 1.x disappears - which is exactly the day the other pin stops.
#
# NO CONFLICT WITH fpms-numpy, BY CONSTRUCTION. Both files use the
# specific form (`Pin: version`), and apt_preferences(5) says the FIRST
# matching specific-form record wins. Files in preferences.d are read in
# alphanumeric order, so "fpms-numpy" is read before "fpms-numpy-ceiling" -
# but it does not matter, because the two version sets are disjoint: fpms-numpy
# matches 1.x only and this file matches 2.x and 3.x only. Keep them disjoint.
# If you ever widen either one, you have created an order-dependent pin, which
# is a bug that reads like a working file.
#
# Four records rather than two because apt's version matcher understands
# exactly one wildcard - a TRAILING `*` - and Debian's numpy carries epoch 1.
# "1:2.0.0" does not begin with "2", so `Pin: version 2.*` does not match it.
# Both spellings, both major versions.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE CANNOT DO: apt has no authority over pip. pip installs into
# /usr/local/lib/python3.10/dist-packages, which PRECEDES apt's
# /usr/lib/python3/dist-packages on sys.path, so a `pip install -U numpy` puts
# a 2.x in front of apt's 1.21.5 for every FPMS service while apt continues to
# report 1.21.5 installed and every apt-side check here still passes. The pip
# half of this policy is /etc/pip.conf -> /etc/fpms/pip-constraints.txt,
# written by stage 20. Both halves are needed; neither can cover for the
# other. See docs/UPDATING.md.

Explanation: FPMS-OS: NumPy 2.x breaks every ROS Humble C extension in this image (1.x ABI).
Package: python3-numpy
Pin: version 2.*
Pin-Priority: -1

Explanation: FPMS-OS: same, spelled with Debian's epoch 1 - apt matches the version string exactly as it prints it.
Package: python3-numpy
Pin: version 1:2.*
Pin-Priority: -1

Explanation: FPMS-OS: a 3.x would break the 1.x ABI at least as hard as 2.x did.
Package: python3-numpy
Pin: version 3.*
Pin-Priority: -1

Explanation: FPMS-OS: same, with the epoch.
Package: python3-numpy
Pin: version 1:3.*
Pin-Priority: -1
